  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-6}{(x-9)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-9}+\\frac{B}{(x-9)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A\\cdot B?$",
  "worked_solution": "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-9)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6 & =A(x-9)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=9$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(9)-6 & =A(9-9)+B \\\\ 21 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)-6 & =A(0-9)+21 \\\\ -6 & =-9A+21 \\\\ 9A & =27 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B=3\\cdot 21=63.\n$$",
